---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { site, incorporation } from '../config/site';

const title = 'Privacy policy';
const lead =
  'What personal information the Society collects, how it is used and protected, and the choices and rights you have.';
---

<Page title={title} eyebrow="Governance" lead={lead}>
  <p class="text-small text-muted">Last updated: 20 October 2026.</p>

  <h2>1. About this policy</h2>
  <p>
    The Society of Natural Capital Accounting (&ldquo;SoNCA&rdquo;, &ldquo;the Society&rdquo;,
    &ldquo;we&rdquo;, &ldquo;us&rdquo; or &ldquo;our&rdquo;) is a not-for-profit professional
    association incorporated in the Australian Capital Territory under the <em>{incorporation.act}</em>{
      incorporation.number ? ` (Association No. ${incorporation.number})` : ''
    }.
  </p>
  <p>
    This policy explains what personal information we collect through our website at
    naturalcapitalaccounting.org and in the course of our activities, how we use and protect it, and
    the choices and rights you have. It applies to members, prospective members, event participants,
    subscribers and general visitors to our website.
  </p>
  <p>
    We respect your privacy and handle personal information in a way that is consistent with the
    Australian Privacy Principles in the <em>Privacy Act 1988</em> (Cth). As a small not-for-profit,
    some of these obligations may not currently apply to us as a matter of law, but we have chosen to
    follow them as a matter of good practice. Because the Society has members and correspondents
    internationally, we also aim to meet the expectations of comparable frameworks such as the
    European Union General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) where they apply to you.
  </p>

  <h2>2. What information we collect</h2>
  <p>
    We only collect personal information that is reasonably necessary for our activities as a
    professional association.
  </p>

  <h3>Information you provide to us</h3>
  <p>This includes information you give us when you:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      join or renew your membership: your name, email address, billing address, organisation (if you
      give one), the class of membership, and for the student concession the details we ask for to
      confirm it, such as the institution where you study. Payment is processed by Stripe (see section
      8); we do not see or store your card details;
    </li>
    <li>
      complete our optional welcome form after joining, where every question is optional;
    </li>
    <li>
      register for a seminar, the annual meeting or another event: your name, email address and the
      other details on the registration form;
    </li>
    <li>
      propose a seminar or working group, or speak at a seminar: the details on our proposal forms and
      speaker declaration, including, for speakers, your position, a photo, a short description and
      your declaration of interests;
    </li>
    <li>take part in a seminar or meeting that is recorded (see section 3);</li>
    <li>ask to join our mailing list; or</li>
    <li>
      contact us by email, in which case we collect your name, email address and the contents of your
      message.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Information collected automatically</h3>
  <p>
    Like most websites, our hosting provider, Cloudflare, automatically records standard technical
    information, including your IP address, browser type, the pages you view, referring pages and the
    date and time of access. This is used to keep the site secure and running properly. We do not use
    a website analytics service.
  </p>

  <h3>Cookies</h3>
  <p>
    We do not use advertising or tracking cookies. Our hosting provider may set a cookie needed for the
    security of the site. You can disable or delete cookies through your browser settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site stores one item locally in your browser: your choice of light or dark theme. It is not
    sent to us and is not used to identify you.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not seek to collect sensitive information (such as health, racial or political information)
    and ask that you do not send it to us.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Seminars, meetings and recordings</h2>
  <p>
    The Society's seminars are recorded, and in the foundation year the recordings are published on
    our website, where anyone can watch them. A recording may include the name, image and voice of
    anyone who speaks or has their camera on, and questions or comments posted in the chat. We tell
    participants at the start of each seminar that it is being recorded. If you do not want to appear
    in a recording, you can keep your camera off, change the name you display, or send your question
    to us by email instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Speakers agree, on their speaker declaration, to the Society publishing the recording, their
    slides, their photo, a short description of them and their declaration of interests. These are
    shown on a speakers slide and alongside the seminar on our website.
  </p>
  <p>
    Committee and other Society meetings may be recorded to help prepare the minutes. Those recordings
    are not published, and those present are told at the start.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The register of members</h2>
  <p>
    As an incorporated association, the Society must keep a register of its members. The Secretary
    keeps it. It records each member's name and contact details, class of membership, the date they
    became a member, the date their membership ends, and anything else the Act or the rules require.
  </p>
  <p>
    Under the Act, members may ask to inspect the register, and the committee may refuse a request
    that is not related to the management or purposes of the Society. A member may also ask that
    access to their personal details in the register be restricted. To make either request, contact
    the Public Officer (see section 14).
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not publish a list of members. If we create a member directory, being listed in it will be
    optional and only with your consent.
  </p>

  <h2>5. The Society's use of AI tools</h2>
  <p>
    The Society may use AI tools to help draft documents and to transcribe or summarise meetings and
    seminars. When a meeting or seminar is recorded or transcribed, participants are told at the
    start. We do not put members' personal information or confidential committee papers into AI tools
    that use them to train models, and a person checks anything produced with AI before the Society
    publishes it.
  </p>

  <h2>6. How we use your information</h2>
  <p>We use personal information to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>process and administer membership, including verifying eligibility and any concession claimed;</li>
    <li>keep the register of members;</li>
    <li>collect fees and issue receipts;</li>
    <li>provide member services and run our seminars, events and the annual meeting;</li>
    <li>publish seminar recordings, slides and speakers' declarations of interests;</li>
    <li>assess proposals for seminars and working groups;</li>
    <li>
      send members notices of meetings and information about the Society's activities, and send
      newsletters to those who ask for them (you can unsubscribe at any time);
    </li>
    <li>respond to your enquiries; and</li>
    <li>
      meet our governance, record-keeping and legal obligations as an incorporated association.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We will only use your information for the purpose for which it was provided, or for a directly
    related purpose you would reasonably expect, unless you consent otherwise or we are required or
    permitted by law.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Consent and choice</h2>
  <p>
    By providing information to us you consent to us handling it as described in this policy. You can
    withdraw consent or change your preferences at any time — for example by unsubscribing from a
    mailing, or by contacting us using the details in section 14. Withdrawing consent may affect our
    ability to provide membership or services to you.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Disclosure and third parties</h2>
  <p>We do not sell, rent or trade your personal information.</p>
  <p>
    We share personal information only with trusted service providers who help us operate, and only
    to the extent needed for them to perform their service. These may include providers of:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>website hosting, which stores site files and server logs;</li>
    <li>payment processing, for membership and event payments;</li>
    <li>email and mailing services, which deliver newsletters and manage subscriptions;</li>
    <li>form and contact services, which deliver messages you send us; and</li>
    <li>analytics, which provide aggregate visitor statistics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These providers are required to handle your information in accordance with their own obligations
    and to use it only for the services they provide to us. We may also disclose personal information
    where required or authorised by law, or to protect the rights, property or safety of the Society,
    our members or others.
  </p>

  <p>The providers we currently use are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong>, which hosts this website and keeps standard server logs;</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stripe</strong>, which processes membership and event payments. Card details go
      directly to Stripe and are not seen or stored by the Society;
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Humanitix</strong>, which manages registration for our seminars and other events. When
      you register, Humanitix collects your name, email address and the other details on its
      registration form, and makes them available to the Society;
    </li>
    <li><strong>Zoho Mail</strong>, which hosts the Society's email;</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Microsoft Teams</strong>, which we use to hold online seminars and meetings, and which
      makes the recordings; and
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>YouTube</strong>, which hosts the seminar recordings linked from our website.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We use event registrations to run the event and to send registrants information about it, such as
    joining details, the recording and the slides. We do not give the names or contact details of
    registrants to speakers or to other organisations.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not currently use a mailing list service, web forms or a website analytics service. If we
    start using one, we will name it here.
  </p>
  <p>
    The register of members and other Society records are kept in files held by the Secretary and
    other office bearers, using the storage services of their own organisations or accounts.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Overseas disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    Some of our service providers may store or process information on servers located outside
    Australia. Where this occurs we take reasonable steps to ensure your information is handled
    consistently with this policy. As the Society grows internationally, we will keep our
    arrangements for cross-border handling of personal information under review. Cloudflare, Stripe,
    Microsoft, YouTube (Google) and Zoho may process information outside Australia, including in the
    United States.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Storage, security and retention</h2>
  <p>
    We take reasonable steps to protect personal information from misuse, loss, and unauthorised
    access, modification or disclosure. We hold most information electronically through the providers
    described above.
  </p>
  <p>
    We keep personal information only for as long as it is needed for the purposes described in this
    policy, or as required for our records as an incorporated association and to meet legal
    obligations. When information is no longer needed, we take reasonable steps to destroy or
    de-identify it.
  </p>

  <h2>11. External links</h2>
  <p>
    Our website contains links to external sites that we do not operate, including partner
    organisations and technical resources such as the UN System of Environmental-Economic Accounting.
    We have no control over, and are not responsible for, the content or privacy practices of those
    sites. We encourage you to read the privacy policy of any site you visit.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You may request access to the personal information we hold about you, and ask us to correct it if
    it is inaccurate, out of date or incomplete. You can also ask us to stop sending you
    communications. To make a request, contact us using the details in section 14. We will respond
    within a reasonable period. There is generally no charge for making a request, though a
    reasonable cost may apply in some cases.
  </p>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live, you may have additional rights — for example under the Australian
    Privacy Principles or, if you are in the European Union or United Kingdom, under the GDPR,
    including rights to erasure and to object to certain processing. We will honour these rights
    where they apply to you.
  </p>

  <h2>13. Complaints</h2>
  <p>
    If you have a concern about how we have handled your personal information, please
    {' '}<a href="/complaints/">contact us</a> first so we can try to resolve it. If you are not satisfied
    with our response, you may be able to lodge a complaint with the Office of the Australian
    Information Commissioner at
    {' '}<a href="https://www.oaic.gov.au" rel="noopener">oaic.gov.au</a>, or with the relevant data
    protection authority in your jurisdiction.
  </p>

  <h2>14. Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    For any questions about this policy, to update your details, or to make a request or complaint,
    please contact:
  </p>
  <p>
    The Public Officer<br />
    Society of Natural Capital Accounting<br />
    <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a><br />
    naturalcapitalaccounting.org
  </p>

  <h2>15. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy from time to time to reflect changes in our activities or in the law.
    The current version will always be available on our website, and we will note the date it was
    last updated at the top of this page.
  </p>
</Page>
